From 324c626a980e140b9e5fedd4a38c793c902e90cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 16:41:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 28/29] CI packages the software driver, and refuses to publish
 one the guest cannot load

A workflow does this because the machine writing the code has no network to Debian and no cross
toolchain, and because the failure mode of a hand-made driver zip is invisible until it costs somebody
an evening: a library the loader cannot open produces an empty Vulkan device list, not an error.

The steps are chosen so each one's failure is a message and not a mystery. The suite index is the
source of truth for which Mesa exists (pick_deb refuses a suite that moved off the pinned 25.0.7 while
naming what it found instead, and refuses to guess between two matching records); every .deb is checked
against the SHA256 the index recorded before it is unpacked; the one edit made to any binary is
`DT_RUNPATH=$ORIGIN`, asserted from the object afterwards rather than trusted from patchelf's exit
code; and the archive's shape is re-read at the end as the importer will read it, so a companion present
in the zip but absent from meta.json - which adopt() refuses on the phone, correctly - fails here.

The sizes are checked against limits read out of DriverArchivePolicy.java rather than copied into this
file, because a gate carrying its own numbers drifts out of agreement with the app it is guarding; if a
constant is ever renamed, the script stops with "could not read" instead of defaulting to something.

What this job deliberately does not do is rule on the guest rootfs: it is the user's image, not
reconstructible here, so the run measures what the libraries demand and records it as minGlibc for the
device-side check added in the previous commit. The header of the file says this in those terms, because
"the CI was green" is otherwise read as "somebody checked whether it loads".

The gate scripts carry 29 cases of their own (tools/lavapipe/test_lavapipe_tools.sh) on fixtures -
including a version string deliberately split across the byte scanner's own 1 MiB chunk, and a report
with no numbers in it, where min_glibc refuses to write a blank minGlibc - and they now run on every
push in gpu-guard-ci.yml, along with :app:testDebugUnitTest for DriverArchivePolicyTest. That last line
matters more than it looks: nothing in any workflow had ever compiled app/src/test, so the importer's
policy tests have been decoration until now. Two of the gate's cases failed on first run and one of them
was the gate being wrong about its own exit code, which is the argument for testing it at all.

Co-authored-by: arena-agent <297053741+arena-agent@users.noreply.github.com>
